      "id": 1,
      "text": "Brownstown was platted on April 8, 1816, even before Indiana achieved statehood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownstown%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Jacob Brown, a War of 1812 general who helped shape early Indiana history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownstown%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Brownstown became Jackson County’s seat in November 1816, replacing Fort Vallonia after only a few months in power.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownstown%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The historic Jackson County Courthouse, now on the National Register, sits proudly in Brownstown’s town square.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownstown%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Brownstown borders the Jackson‑Washington State Forest and lies near the Hoosier National Forest along the East Fork of the White River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownstown%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town’s public library and a private Lutheran elementary‑middle school serve as key educational hubs for residents.",
